      {
        "id": 203558,
        "name": "WalkingTarget",
        "username": "WalkingTarget",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/walkingtarget/{size}/4833_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-09-13T15:25:10.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I think it's pretty obvious that I listen to everything.</p><p>I agree with Foote's definition that \"an Interlude is anything that's not a Tuesday release\" is an apt one, but the name implies that they're less important which might explain lower download rates.</p><p>For spoilers I think I've said before somewhere (maybe over on Reddit) that I think that spoilers only really make sense for story points that are important for a narrative game. In general, the card game doesn't qualify and neither does Tactics (sure there's a lot of story <em>behind the scenes</em>, but the act of discovery of the story isn't the point of the game). The RPG <em>can</em> make sense to mark spoilers, but mostly only for things that are important GM-only info. That is, If it's a big reveal late in an adventure that's meant to blow the players' minds, then it's appropriate for a spoiler tag. If it's part of the opening \"tell the players this\" exposition of a campaign, it's probably not a spoiler.</p><p>Since I listen to it all regardless, maybe my opinions on what's worth marking as a spoiler are less useful for the purposes of actually compartmentalising information that people care about, though.</p>",
